# HelloQnx

A simple toy project to teach getting starting to QNX

## Setting up QNX license

1. Register yourself with qnx.com
1. Setup the license and get the details. Add QNX Software Development Platform 8.0 - Perpetual - Developer License - Non-Commercial Single-User License (Non-Commercial/Academic License Class) (ver. 8.0)
1. Add License key

```sh
~ qlicense -userEmail your@email.com -activateLicenseKey XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX
License key is added: XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX
Info: Remote license activation is not required for this key

~ qlicense -listLicenseKeys
[XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX-XXXX] - Activated - QNX Software Development Platform 8.0 Education
```

## Setting up QNX toolchain

1. Download the SDP and extract it to `/path/qnx/sdp/`

1. Source the sdp and to compile your code

```sh
. ~/qnx800/qnxsdp-env.sh
QNX_HOST=~/qnx800~/host/linux/x86_64
QNX_TARGET=~/qnx800/target/qnx
MAKEFLAGS=-I~/qnx800/target/qnx/usr/include
```

## Cross compilation of QNX

Source the QNX SDP environment (the script must be sourced from bash),
configure CMake with the QNX toolchain, and build the executable:

```sh
bash
. ~/qnx800/qnxsdp-env.sh

cmake -S . -B build/cmake-aarch64le-debug -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=cmake/qnx-toolchain.cmake -DQNX_ARCH=aarch64le -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
cmake --build build/cmake-aarch64le-debug

file build/cmake-aarch64le-debug/HelloQnx
build/cmake-aarch64le-debug/HelloQnx: ELF 64-bit LSB pie executable, ARM aarch64, version 1 (SYSV), dynamically linked, interpreter /usr/lib/ldqnx-64.so.2, with debug_info, not stripped
```

To build for another QNX architecture, set `QNX_ARCH` to the corresponding
processor name, such as `x86_64`.

## Nix development environment

The Nix shell provides CMake and Ninja. The QNX SDP remains installed outside
Nix because it requires QNX licensing.

```sh
bash
. ~/qnx800/qnxsdp-env.sh
nix develop

cmake -S . -B build/cmake-aarch64le-debug -G Ninja -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=cmake/qnx-toolchain.cmake -DQNX_ARCH=aarch64le -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
cmake --build build/cmake-aarch64le-debug
```

The flake uses the NixOS 24.05 package set. Commit both `flake.nix` and the
generated `flake.lock` so every team member receives the same tool versions.

## Raspberry Pi 5 Linux cross compilation

Enter the Nix environment and cross-compile for 64-bit Linux on Raspberry Pi 5:

```sh
nix develop

cmake -S . -B build/raspberry-pi-5-debug -G Ninja -DCMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE=cmake/raspberry-pi-5-linux-toolchain.cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
cmake --build build/raspberry-pi-5-debug

file build/raspberry-pi-5-debug/HelloQnx
```
